A||||9||kslish||Zanardi critically injured in CART crash||||1||
Z||000000||kslish||09-16-2001||03:54 AM||||I don't know how many people follow the CART open wheel racing series on the forum (especially in wake of this weeks national tragedies) but since the German CART race will not be telivised until 1pm Monday on ESPN, I thought I'd update any CART fans on some unfortunate news that occurred today.  <P>During the American Memorial 500 (renamed in rememberance of the lives lost in the USA) in Germany, Alex Zanardi lost control of his car coming out of the pits on cold tires while leading the race with 12 laps to go.  His car apparently slid sideways across the track and was t-boned by Alex Tagliani who was traveling at approximately 200mph and had no time to slow down or try to avoid a collision.  Tagliani survived with relatively minor injuries but Zanardi was not as fortunate.  His car was sheared in two by the impact and Zanardi's legs were apparently crushed.  They had to be amputated in a multiple hour surgical procedure.  He also suffered extreme blood loss due to the injury, so he isn't out of the woods yet.  <P>Terrible to see something like this happen again to another of the world's best racers.  <P>Ken S. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Galen||09-16-2001||04:28 AM||||Unfortunately it does happen most often to the best, or we just pay closer attention to it when it is the best that it happens to. In the weeks following the WTC and Pentagon attacks, this in most peoples lives will take a back seat. But He does have my hopes & wishes for a speedy and complete(to the best he can without legs) recovery. I just read about this on Yahoo, saddens me.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/g-flag-sig.gif"> <FONT COLOR="purple">1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe</FONT> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/g-flagnc-sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||09-16-2001||06:02 AM||||Wow..that totally sucks; he was an awesome driver.  Hmm...I guess we can think positively...maybe he'll try hard to get back into racing using drive-by-wire controls; they have all hand controls for handicapped people, but I don't know if they could pull it off inside such tight quarters.  Something like that could make huge strides in developing the technology to incorporate it into production cars, eventually.  Too bad such a horrible thing had to happen to such an excellent person.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||09-16-2001||07:10 AM||||There is a woman in I believe Trans Am or Grand American Series, that is a paraplegic and races a Ferrari 355 with all hand controls.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||09-16-2001||07:57 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>...Hmm...I guess we can think positively...maybe he'll try hard to get back into racing using drive-by-wire controls; ...Something like that could make huge strides in developing the technology to incorporate it into production cars, eventually...Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Dude, that's THE most positive thing I've heard about ANYTHING in a VERY long time. <P>Two BIG thumbs up!<P><BR>Oh, and Roger, I think she's in Trans Am. VERY cool!<P><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
